issue.body:
Hi,

I find the widths are inconsistent when UTF8 characters are used. I'm not sure whether it's caused by the character display of the terminal or the library implementation.

Example:

```cpp
#include <fmt/format.h>

auto main() -> int {
    fmt::println("|{:^20}|", 12345);
    fmt::println("|{:^20}|", "normal string");
    fmt::println("|{:^20}|", "❌");
    fmt::println("|{:^20}|", "✅");
    fmt::println("|{:^20}|", "Müller");
    fmt::println("|{:^20}|", "我");
    return 0;
}
```
Printouts:

```text
|       12345        |
|   normal string    |
|         ❌          |
|         ✅          |
|       Müller       |
|         我         |
```

[Godbolt link](https://compiler-explorer.com/z/cEWdv8Eob)

On `main` (e4751cc70), this fails to compile with GCC 15.2 and `-std=c++20`. It compiles and runs correctly with fmt 12.2.0.

Compiler Explorer: https://godbolt.org/z/hxMdoozx8

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <fmt/compile.h>
#include <fmt/ranges.h>

int main() {
  std::array<int, 3> values{1, 2, 3};
  auto s = fmt::format(FMT_COMPILE("array={}"), values);
  fmt::print("{}\n", s);
}
```

Expected output:
`array=[1, 2, 3]`

It works without `FMT_COMPILE`.

This issue seems to have been introduced in 61e3b923.

A branch in `nested_format_specs::write` attempts to construct an iterator-specific `FormatContext` from a `basic_appender<char>`, which cannot be converted to a `std::back_insert_iterator<std::string>` as required by the `generic_context<std::back_insert_iterator<std::string>, char>` constructor. This example never actually tries to construct the `FormatContext` (as the branch is for padding, and we don't specify a width), but it still needs to be instantiated.

Using this test program:

```c++
#include <chrono>
#include <print>

#include <fmt/format.h>
#include <fmt/chrono.h>

using namespace std::chrono;

int main()
{
    // one second past the epoch
    std::time_t t = 1;
    auto systime = time_point_cast<microseconds>(system_clock::from_time_t(t));
    systime += microseconds(123456);
    auto zt{zoned_time("Etc/GMT+4", systime)};
    auto stime = zt.get_local_time();
    auto us = floor<milliseconds>(stime);

    std::print("std = {:%S}\n", us);
    fmt::print("fmt = {:%S}\n", us);

    return 0;
}
```

Using C++23 (GCC 16) and the latest fmt checked out from main HEAD today, I get:

```
$ g++ -I$HOME/src/fmt/include -std=gnu++23 -o fmttest fmttest.cpp $HOME/src/fmt/libfmt.a
$ ./fmttest
std = 01.123
fmt = 02.18446744073709550739
```

The std::print() formatting is correct; the fmt::print() formatting is wrong.

Using `FMT_COMPILE` (or the `_cf` literal) with user-defined type formatted via `format_as` fails to compile.

Defining a `formatter` specialization works (albeit with a generic context param, as I learned from #4720; and now I see it's clearly written in the doc 😅 ).

Still, I see no mention that types with `format_as` should not be supported.

Example program:
```c++
#include <fmt/format.h>
#include <fmt/compile.h>

struct Foo { int a; };
int format_as(Foo foo) { return foo.a; }

std::string foo(Foo a) {
    return fmt::format(FMT_COMPILE("{}"), a);
}
```

Compiler Explorer repro: https://godbolt.org/z/f9Gf8r65n
Version: 12.0.0 / trunk
